WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.452
<v Maya>New VC fund trying to break through.

2
00:00:03.452 --> 00:00:07.863
<v Maya>That, and four more stories, on The Breakdown.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.659
<v Alex>This is Bits and Tickers.

4
00:00:10.659 --> 00:00:13.048
<v Alex>What got built, and what it's worth.

5
00:00:13.048 --> 00:00:13.645
<v Alex>I'm Alex.

6
00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:15.273
<v Maya>And I'm Maya.

7
00:00:15.273 --> 00:00:19.936
<v Maya>It's Thursday, November 12th, and this is The Breakdown: fewer stories,

8
00:00:19.936 --> 00:00:21.971
<v Maya>with time to dig into each one.

9
00:00:21.971 --> 00:00:25.452
<v Maya>Everything we talk about is linked in the show notes.

10
00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.284
<v Alex>On today's show.

11
00:00:27.284 --> 00:00:29.664
<v Alex>New VC fund trying to break through.

12
00:00:29.664 --> 00:00:32.243
<v Alex>AI revenue numbers just went ballistic.

13
00:00:32.243 --> 00:00:34.821
<v Alex>AI characters you can actually talk to.

14
00:00:34.821 --> 00:00:37.400
<v Alex>AI solves a really old quantum problem.

15
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.036
<v Alex>And Nvidia's AI financing is getting serious attention.

16
00:00:41.036 --> 00:00:42.226
<v Alex>Let's get into it.

17
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.667
<v Maya>Time for The Ticker.

18
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:50.335
<v Maya>At Wednesday's close, the S&amp;P 500 finished at 7,619, up 1.3 percent.

19
00:00:50.335 --> 00:00:56.253
<v Maya>The Nasdaq Composite was up 1.6 percent and the Dow was up 0.5 percent.

20
00:00:57.241 --> 00:01:01.916
<v Alex>And in big tech, Tesla had the best day, up 2.9 percent,

21
00:01:01.916 --> 00:01:06.508
<v Alex>while Microsoft was at the other end, down 2.3 percent.

22
00:01:06.508 --> 00:01:09.012
<v Alex>That's a snapshot, not advice.

23
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.840
<v Alex>TechCrunch has a piece on this guy, Olaf Sakkers,

24
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.927
<v Alex>who's trying to raise a second fund.

25
00:01:15.615 --> 00:01:15.963
<v Maya>Right.

26
00:01:15.963 --> 00:01:20.542
<v Maya>Apparently, it’s really hard to get in as a first-time fund manager these days.

27
00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:21.812
<v Alex>Seriously.

28
00:01:21.812 --> 00:01:26.535
<v Alex>PitchBook says established firms are grabbing almost everything – like 88% of the

29
00:01:26.535 --> 00:01:27.760
<v Alex>VC dollars this year.

30
00:01:28.448 --> 00:01:29.091
<v Maya>Since when?

31
00:01:29.091 --> 00:01:31.427
<v Maya>That leaves a tiny sliver for newcomers.

32
00:01:32.115 --> 00:01:32.752
<v Alex>It’s tough.

33
00:01:32.752 --> 00:01:37.385
<v Alex>He started RedBlue Capital in 2021, closed a first fund with about five million.

34
00:01:38.073 --> 00:01:40.869
<v Maya>Now he’s aiming for fifty million in the second.

35
00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:41.510
<v Maya>A big jump.

36
00:01:42.198 --> 00:01:46.193
<v Alex>Seems he’s been building trust, letting results speak for themselves,

37
00:01:46.193 --> 00:01:47.641
<v Alex>which he says takes time.

38
00:01:48.328 --> 00:01:51.571
<v Maya>He writes smaller checks, fifty to one hundred thousand,

39
00:01:51.571 --> 00:01:53.656
<v Maya>usually joining deals led by others.

40
00:01:54.344 --> 00:01:58.797
<v Alex>It’s interesting that he’s focusing on what he calls “the real economy,” like

41
00:01:58.797 --> 00:02:00.359
<v Alex>robotics and manufacturing.

42
00:02:01.047 --> 00:02:03.878
<v Maya>So, how does he actually get those LPs to commit,

43
00:02:03.878 --> 00:02:07.807
<v Maya>especially when the established firms are hoovering up all the cash?

44
00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:10.296
<v Alex>Apparently, it’s a trust thing.

45
00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:14.768
<v Alex>He says institutional investors are risk-averse, built to avoid zero returns,

46
00:02:14.768 --> 00:02:16.859
<v Alex>so they stick with the known brands.

47
00:02:17.547 --> 00:02:17.891
<v Maya>Go on.

48
00:02:18.578 --> 00:02:22.576
<v Alex>He had to prove it out with the first fund, backed by family offices,

49
00:02:22.576 --> 00:02:25.589
<v Alex>then gradually bring in institutions for the second.

50
00:02:25.589 --> 00:02:28.891
<v Alex>He’s deliberately letting the performance do the talking.

51
00:02:29.578 --> 00:02:30.700
<v Maya>That’s a long game.

52
00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.591
<v Maya>What was his background, anyway?

53
00:02:32.591 --> 00:02:33.359
<v Maya>Anthropology?

54
00:02:34.047 --> 00:02:37.631
<v Alex>Yeah, Princeton anthropology, a thesis on HIV in South Africa.

55
00:02:37.631 --> 00:02:42.199
<v Alex>Then he was drafted into the Israeli military – desk job liaising with the U.S.

56
00:02:42.199 --> 00:02:46.766
<v Alex>military, interestingly enough – and got into autonomous vehicles through that.

57
00:02:47.453 --> 00:02:48.412
<v Maya>Hold on, that's…

58
00:02:48.412 --> 00:02:48.771
<v Maya>a lot.

59
00:02:49.458 --> 00:02:49.807
<v Alex>It is.

60
00:02:49.807 --> 00:02:53.239
<v Alex>He was hired by Michael Granoff, who had capital to invest,

61
00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:57.426
<v Alex>and they built the first fund almost by accident, learning as they went.

62
00:02:57.426 --> 00:03:00.974
<v Alex>Granoff had been an early backer of Better Place, by the way.

63
00:03:01.661 --> 00:03:01.895
<v Maya>Huh.

64
00:03:01.895 --> 00:03:04.411
<v Maya>It sounds like he's leveraging connections.

65
00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:08.905
<v Alex>I mean, it's a smart strategy, letting the results do the talking.

66
00:03:08.905 --> 00:03:13.750
<v Alex>It’s a lot less stressful than chasing the big institutional commitments right away.

67
00:03:14.438 --> 00:03:16.940
<v Maya>It sounds like a lot of luck to me, though.

68
00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.303
<v Maya>Getting hired on the spot, building a fund "almost by accident"— that’s not

69
00:03:21.303 --> 00:03:21.943
<v Maya>repeatable.

70
00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.767
<v Alex>Maybe not, but he clearly has something – connections,

71
00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:27.801
<v Alex>a knack for spotting opportunities.

72
00:03:27.801 --> 00:03:30.938
<v Alex>Those five million dollars had to come from somewhere.

73
00:03:31.625 --> 00:03:33.487
<v Maya>I think it's more about inertia.

74
00:03:33.487 --> 00:03:36.222
<v Maya>Established funds have this gravitational pull,

75
00:03:36.222 --> 00:03:39.073
<v Maya>and it’s incredibly hard to break free from that.

76
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.202
<v Alex>True, but he seems to understand the game.

77
00:03:42.202 --> 00:03:44.179
<v Alex>Small checks, letting others lead,

78
00:03:44.179 --> 00:03:47.552
<v Alex>providing support— it's a way to get his foot in the door.

79
00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.614
<v Maya>It’s still a long shot, though, isn't it?

80
00:03:50.614 --> 00:03:53.510
<v Maya>Getting to fifty million after starting with five?

81
00:03:54.198 --> 00:03:56.146
<v Alex>It's a test of endurance, he says.

82
00:03:56.833 --> 00:04:01.447
<v Maya>My read is that he's banking on the "gravity of perception" working in his favor

83
00:04:01.447 --> 00:04:05.312
<v Maya>eventually, but I suspect that’s the most fragile part of his plan.

84
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.426
<v Maya>Apparently, OpenAI crossed $10 billion in quarterly revenue.

85
00:04:09.426 --> 00:04:11.196
<v Maya>That’s according to TechCrunch.

86
00:04:11.874 --> 00:04:12.664
<v Alex>Hold on, what?

87
00:04:13.342 --> 00:04:16.079
<v Maya>Yeah, a nearly 50% jump from the second quarter.

88
00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:19.159
<v Maya>It’s a significant increase in their growth rate, too.

89
00:04:19.837 --> 00:04:22.435
<v Alex>Still, they weren't exactly struggling before.

90
00:04:23.113 --> 00:04:25.618
<v Maya>They were already growing at 18% previously.

91
00:04:25.618 --> 00:04:29.946
<v Maya>Their adjusted operating margin improved slightly, too, from -150% to -109%.

92
00:04:30.624 --> 00:04:31.189
<v Alex>That fast?

93
00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:35.511
<v Maya>They’re now talking about a $50 billion annual revenue run rate.

94
00:04:35.511 --> 00:04:38.813
<v Maya>Anthropic, meanwhile, is claiming an $81 billion run rate.

95
00:04:39.491 --> 00:04:42.597
<v Alex>Their calculation methods are different, though, right?

96
00:04:43.275 --> 00:04:43.737
<v Maya>Exactly.

97
00:04:43.737 --> 00:04:46.044
<v Maya>They count sales through cloud partners,

98
00:04:46.044 --> 00:04:48.640
<v Maya>while OpenAI doesn't for some of their sales.

99
00:04:49.318 --> 00:04:52.399
<v Alex>What’s driving this sudden acceleration, do you think?

100
00:04:52.399 --> 00:04:54.909
<v Alex>It sounds like a lot to pull out of nowhere.

101
00:04:55.587 --> 00:05:00.247
<v Maya>Apparently, they’ve been focused on tighter pricing, discounts, and better models.

102
00:05:00.247 --> 00:05:04.510
<v Maya>They're also building more enterprise-focused products and these AI agents.

103
00:05:05.188 --> 00:05:05.589
<v Alex>Really?

104
00:05:05.589 --> 00:05:08.972
<v Alex>I thought they were lagging behind Anthropic in that space.

105
00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:14.090
<v Maya>They were, but they’ve been trying to catch up, shifting focus to those areas.

106
00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.822
<v Maya>It’s interesting that they’re pulling ahead now.

107
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.792
<v Alex>Just a slight improvement in operating margin though - still a massive loss.

108
00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.490
<v Maya>It narrowed from a 150% negative margin to 109%, yes.

109
00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.965
<v Maya>It's a small shift, but in that scale it’s still significant.

110
00:05:29.643 --> 00:05:31.160
<v Alex>$81 billion for Anthropic?

111
00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:31.393
<v Alex>Wow.

112
00:05:32.071 --> 00:05:35.150
<v Maya>That’s their annual revenue run rate, per their claim,

113
00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:38.058
<v Maya>and it’s due to those sales through cloud partners.

114
00:05:38.735 --> 00:05:43.354
<v Maya>I think it’s a bit misleading to get excited about this run rate stuff, honestly.

115
00:05:43.354 --> 00:05:44.722
<v Maya>It’s just a calculation.

116
00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:45.869
<v Alex>Hold on.

117
00:05:45.869 --> 00:05:46.690
<v Alex>Fifty billion?

118
00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:48.449
<v Alex>That’s still a lot of revenue.

119
00:05:49.127 --> 00:05:51.977
<v Maya>It is, but it’s still burning a ton of cash, Alex.

120
00:05:51.977 --> 00:05:55.170
<v Maya>That operating margin narrowing isn't exactly a victory.

121
00:05:55.848 --> 00:06:00.465
<v Alex>True, but they are shifting focus to enterprise, and that seems to be paying off,

122
00:06:00.465 --> 00:06:02.004
<v Alex>at least according to this.

123
00:06:02.682 --> 00:06:03.768
<v Maya>I’m less convinced.

124
00:06:03.768 --> 00:06:07.426
<v Maya>It sounds like they’re just trying to catch up to Anthropic now.

125
00:06:08.103 --> 00:06:12.452
<v Alex>I'm not so sure they're "catching up." They're just taking a different route.

126
00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:17.855
<v Maya>Maybe, but I still think the hype around these run rates is obscuring the fact that

127
00:06:17.855 --> 00:06:20.246
<v Maya>they're still losing money hand over fist.

128
00:06:20.924 --> 00:06:25.306
<v Alex>Ultimately, it just shows how much demand there is for what they're building,

129
00:06:25.306 --> 00:06:28.322
<v Alex>even if the current business model isn't sustainable.

130
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.484
<v Alex>So, Adobe’s been showing off some experimental stuff at their Max conference – did

131
00:06:33.484 --> 00:06:35.562
<v Alex>you see anything that caught your eye?

132
00:06:36.212 --> 00:06:37.753
<v Maya>Project Screen Test, mostly.

133
00:06:37.753 --> 00:06:42.211
<v Maya>Apparently, you can now have conversations with AI-generated characters to, like,

134
00:06:42.211 --> 00:06:43.642
<v Maya>shape their personalities.

135
00:06:44.292 --> 00:06:45.821
<v Alex>Since when is that a thing?

136
00:06:45.821 --> 00:06:46.331
<v Alex>Sounds...

137
00:06:46.331 --> 00:06:46.841
<v Alex>involved.

138
00:06:47.492 --> 00:06:49.909
<v Maya>It's designed to give creators more control.

139
00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:52.326
<v Maya>You start by giving them personality traits,

140
00:06:52.326 --> 00:06:55.951
<v Maya>then chat with them to flesh out their backstory, goals, all that.

141
00:06:56.602 --> 00:06:59.910
<v Alex>And it generates animated tests based on those conversations?

142
00:07:00.561 --> 00:07:01.003
<v Maya>It does.

143
00:07:01.003 --> 00:07:05.538
<v Maya>My read is that it’s all about addressing the lack of creative control in AI video

144
00:07:05.538 --> 00:07:06.146
<v Maya>generation.

145
00:07:06.797 --> 00:07:08.369
<v Alex>Sounds like a bunch of demos.

146
00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:09.905
<v Maya>Still, it feels…

147
00:07:09.905 --> 00:07:10.955
<v Maya>clunky, doesn't it?

148
00:07:10.955 --> 00:07:14.660
<v Maya>I mean, what’s the actual process for giving the character a voice?

149
00:07:15.311 --> 00:07:17.511
<v Alex>You can upload sample audio, apparently.

150
00:07:17.511 --> 00:07:20.427
<v Alex>Or describe it in a prompt - they’ll try to match it.

151
00:07:20.427 --> 00:07:22.957
<v Alex>It’s not like they’re generating from scratch.

152
00:07:23.608 --> 00:07:26.102
<v Maya>So you're basically building a digital puppet?

153
00:07:26.753 --> 00:07:27.193
<v Alex>Kind of.

154
00:07:27.193 --> 00:07:31.644
<v Alex>The article mentioned it’s because existing AI video tools lack creative control;

155
00:07:31.644 --> 00:07:34.887
<v Alex>traditional animation has absolute intention at every step.

156
00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:36.258
<v Maya>Good to know.

157
00:07:36.258 --> 00:07:38.086
<v Maya>Sounds like a neat trick, though.

158
00:07:38.737 --> 00:07:39.729
<v Alex>Definitely a demo.

159
00:07:39.729 --> 00:07:42.099
<v Alex>I'm not seeing how that becomes a workflow.

160
00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:45.808
<v Alex>It's a clever way to inject some personality, I suppose,

161
00:07:45.808 --> 00:07:50.396
<v Alex>but it still feels like a lot of steps for something that could be done more simply.

162
00:07:51.047 --> 00:07:55.435
<v Maya>It's not about being simple; it's about giving creators a tool that wasn't there

163
00:07:55.435 --> 00:07:55.819
<v Maya>before.

164
00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:57.799
<v Alex>But is it a useful tool?

165
00:07:57.799 --> 00:07:59.073
<v Alex>It sounds like a hobby.

166
00:07:59.723 --> 00:08:03.502
<v Maya>I think it addresses a real pain point in the video generation space.

167
00:08:03.502 --> 00:08:05.200
<v Maya>The article explicitly said so.

168
00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:06.187
<v Alex>Maybe.

169
00:08:06.187 --> 00:08:08.036
<v Alex>Still, building a digital puppet…

170
00:08:08.036 --> 00:08:09.213
<v Alex>it’s a weird framing.

171
00:08:09.864 --> 00:08:11.077
<v Maya>It’s a metaphor, Alex.

172
00:08:11.077 --> 00:08:13.226
<v Maya>It's illustrating the level of control.

173
00:08:13.877 --> 00:08:18.052
<v Alex>A metaphor that doesn't really translate to a practical workflow, in my view.

174
00:08:18.703 --> 00:08:21.288
<v Maya>It's a first iteration, and even in that state,

175
00:08:21.288 --> 00:08:24.534
<v Maya>it's demonstrably an improvement over what’s available now,

176
00:08:24.534 --> 00:08:26.349
<v Maya>so it’s already valuable to some.

177
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.596
<v Maya>According to TechCrunch, a startup called PSI used an Anthropic model to crack an

178
00:08:31.596 --> 00:08:33.923
<v Maya>18-year-old problem in quantum computing.

179
00:08:34.598 --> 00:08:35.073
<v Alex>Hang on.

180
00:08:35.073 --> 00:08:35.667
<v Alex>Seriously?

181
00:08:36.343 --> 00:08:39.753
<v Maya>Yeah, a recent MIT grad, David Turturean, apparently did it.

182
00:08:39.753 --> 00:08:42.140
<v Maya>He’s deferred his master’s to work at PSI.

183
00:08:42.815 --> 00:08:43.040
<v Alex>Wow.

184
00:08:43.716 --> 00:08:48.380
<v Maya>The problem has to do with how quickly quantum computers can solve certain issues,

185
00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.964
<v Maya>and Scott Aaronson, one of the original researchers, says he’s…

186
00:08:51.964 --> 00:08:54.353
<v Maya>well, he's got a lot of feelings about it.

187
00:08:55.028 --> 00:08:55.704
<v Alex>Is that all?

188
00:08:56.379 --> 00:09:00.536
<v Maya>It’s a negative result, apparently closing one door on quantum computing,

189
00:09:00.536 --> 00:09:04.977
<v Maya>and PSI’s CEO, Matt Pines, is saying theoretical physics is going to be mostly

190
00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:06.116
<v Maya>machines’ work soon.

191
00:09:06.791 --> 00:09:07.410
<v Alex>Bold claim.

192
00:09:08.085 --> 00:09:12.672
<v Maya>Their current revenue comes from optimizing data centers – simulating things like

193
00:09:12.672 --> 00:09:17.372
<v Maya>heat and power – but the real goal is to turn these breakthroughs into actual tech.

194
00:09:18.047 --> 00:09:19.317
<v Alex>How did he even do it?

195
00:09:19.317 --> 00:09:19.606
<v Alex>Just…

196
00:09:19.606 --> 00:09:24.399
<v Alex>a recent grad and an Anthropic model solving something that’s stumped people for 18

197
00:09:24.399 --> 00:09:24.745
<v Alex>years?

198
00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:27.766
<v Maya>Well, it wasn't just any Anthropic model.

199
00:09:27.766 --> 00:09:29.827
<v Maya>It was Fable 5.2, an unreleased one.

200
00:09:29.827 --> 00:09:34.233
<v Maya>And he used what they’re calling specialized model harnesses and scaffolding,

201
00:09:34.233 --> 00:09:35.607
<v Maya>which apparently is key.

202
00:09:36.282 --> 00:09:36.868
<v Alex>Harnesses?

203
00:09:36.868 --> 00:09:37.746
<v Alex>What are those?

204
00:09:38.421 --> 00:09:38.991
<v Maya>It’s like…

205
00:09:38.991 --> 00:09:43.096
<v Maya>tools and memory systems, helping the model really dig into the problem.

206
00:09:43.096 --> 00:09:47.257
<v Maya>The CEO, Pines, says they need a lot of that to tackle the work properly.

207
00:09:47.933 --> 00:09:49.621
<v Alex>Feels like a demo, doesn’t it?

208
00:09:50.296 --> 00:09:54.680
<v Maya>Aaronson, one of the original researchers, seemed to think so too, initially.

209
00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:58.153
<v Maya>He was surprised it hadn’t shown up in OpenAI’s Mathocalypse,

210
00:09:58.153 --> 00:10:00.146
<v Maya>wondering if it was so much harder.

211
00:10:00.821 --> 00:10:02.791
<v Alex>So, is PSI just doing this for fun?

212
00:10:03.466 --> 00:10:04.154
<v Maya>Not exactly.

213
00:10:04.154 --> 00:10:07.305
<v Maya>They’re currently making money optimizing data centers,

214
00:10:07.305 --> 00:10:11.832
<v Maya>but Pines is pitching the longer-term vision: turning these theoretical physics

215
00:10:11.832 --> 00:10:13.952
<v Maya>breakthroughs into actual technology.

216
00:10:13.952 --> 00:10:18.536
<v Maya>He says he had to stretch to convince investors six to nine months ago that this

217
00:10:18.536 --> 00:10:19.281
<v Maya>was possible.

218
00:10:19.957 --> 00:10:23.154
<v Maya>I think Pines is just playing the investor game, really.

219
00:10:23.154 --> 00:10:26.693
<v Maya>Saying theoretical physics will be mostly machines’ work soon?

220
00:10:26.693 --> 00:10:27.892
<v Maya>That’s a sales pitch.

221
00:10:28.568 --> 00:10:28.798
<v Alex>Hmm.

222
00:10:28.798 --> 00:10:30.750
<v Alex>It’s impressive, though, isn’t it?

223
00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:34.252
<v Alex>A recent grad solving something that’s been open for so long.

224
00:10:34.928 --> 00:10:35.912
<v Maya>Sure, impressive.

225
00:10:35.912 --> 00:10:36.837
<v Maya>But it's a demo.

226
00:10:36.837 --> 00:10:39.036
<v Maya>Aaronson himself seemed to think that.

227
00:10:39.712 --> 00:10:41.983
<v Alex>But the harnesses and scaffolding thing…

228
00:10:41.983 --> 00:10:46.128
<v Alex>that does suggest something more than just throwing a model at a problem.

229
00:10:46.803 --> 00:10:49.657
<v Maya>Maybe, but ultimately, it’s still just one result.

230
00:10:49.657 --> 00:10:54.280
<v Maya>They're chasing a long game, and data center optimization is the real money-maker

231
00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:54.851
<v Maya>right now.

232
00:10:55.527 --> 00:11:00.093
<v Alex>That's true, but the fact that they were able to crack something so fundamental,

233
00:11:00.093 --> 00:11:01.806
<v Alex>even with a specialized setup…

234
00:11:01.806 --> 00:11:03.575
<v Alex>it indicates a potential shift.

235
00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:08.021
<v Maya>I’m not convinced it’s a shift; it's a carefully managed narrative.

236
00:11:08.696 --> 00:11:11.539
<v Alex>Still, someone built something that could do this.

237
00:11:11.539 --> 00:11:14.325
<v Alex>And that’s a worthwhile accomplishment in itself.

238
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.239
<v Alex>This CNBC piece about Nvidia being, essentially, the central bank of AI,

239
00:11:19.239 --> 00:11:23.832
<v Alex>caught my eye because it’s a fascinating way to think about how interconnected

240
00:11:23.832 --> 00:11:25.127
<v Alex>everything has become.

241
00:11:25.127 --> 00:11:30.014
<v Alex>Apparently, they’ve made huge financial commitments to other firms to help grow the

242
00:11:30.014 --> 00:11:30.544
<v Alex>industry.

243
00:11:31.241 --> 00:11:32.502
<v Maya>Hundreds of billions?

244
00:11:32.502 --> 00:11:32.923
<v Maya>That’s…

245
00:11:33.619 --> 00:11:38.305
<v Alex>Yeah, and it’s got the Federal Reserve and other financial institutions watching

246
00:11:38.305 --> 00:11:41.293
<v Alex>closely, just in case something unexpected happens.

247
00:11:41.293 --> 00:11:45.393
<v Alex>They’re worried about a "broad-based repricing of assets," apparently.

248
00:11:46.089 --> 00:11:50.149
<v Maya>Hold on, so is someone saying we’re headed for another 2008 situation?

249
00:11:50.845 --> 00:11:52.724
<v Alex>Sheila Bair, a former regulator,

250
00:11:52.724 --> 00:11:56.364
<v Alex>says Nvidia isn’t "too big to fail" in the same way a bank is.

251
00:11:56.364 --> 00:12:00.357
<v Alex>They still make a product people want, and have a big equity buffer.

252
00:12:01.053 --> 00:12:01.763
<v Maya>Makes sense.

253
00:12:01.763 --> 00:12:06.498
<v Maya>But she does say regulators should still watch for exposure across the financial

254
00:12:06.498 --> 00:12:06.912
<v Maya>system.

255
00:12:07.608 --> 00:12:10.546
<v Alex>And then there's the whole FBI and Congress thing,

256
00:12:10.546 --> 00:12:13.837
<v Alex>reportedly concerning Huang's relationship with someone.

257
00:12:13.837 --> 00:12:16.540
<v Alex>The board still has confidence in him, though.

258
00:12:17.236 --> 00:12:19.440
<v Maya>It’s a lot to juggle, even for Nvidia.

259
00:12:20.136 --> 00:12:23.583
<v Maya>So, how exactly does this "central bank of AI" thing work?

260
00:12:23.583 --> 00:12:23.880
<v Maya>Just…

261
00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:24.950
<v Maya>handing out money?

262
00:12:25.646 --> 00:12:26.352
<v Alex>Pretty much.

263
00:12:26.352 --> 00:12:30.647
<v Alex>They’re providing financial commitments, essentially loans or guarantees,

264
00:12:30.647 --> 00:12:34.530
<v Alex>to other companies to help them build out their AI infrastructure.

265
00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:37.943
<v Alex>It's not just cash, it's a whole web of financial support.

266
00:12:38.639 --> 00:12:42.931
<v Maya>Hold on, what happens if those companies can’t pay back those commitments?

267
00:12:43.627 --> 00:12:44.633
<v Alex>That’s the worry.

268
00:12:44.633 --> 00:12:46.586
<v Alex>If Nvidia’s GPU chip values drop,

269
00:12:46.586 --> 00:12:51.142
<v Alex>it could trigger a chain reaction where companies struggle to repay what they

270
00:12:51.142 --> 00:12:52.385
<v Alex>borrowed to buy them.

271
00:12:53.081 --> 00:12:53.510
<v Maya>That’s…

272
00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:54.183
<v Maya>concerning.

273
00:12:54.879 --> 00:12:59.569
<v Alex>Sheila Bair pointed out that even though Nvidia isn't structurally the same as a

274
00:12:59.569 --> 00:13:04.376
<v Alex>bank, that ripple effect is what regulators are paying attention to – the exposure

275
00:13:04.376 --> 00:13:05.783
<v Alex>across the wider system.

276
00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.713
<v Maya>And the Huang situation complicates things, doesn’t it?

277
00:13:09.713 --> 00:13:10.888
<v Maya>It’s a weird timing.

278
00:13:11.584 --> 00:13:12.649
<v Alex>It certainly does.

279
00:13:12.649 --> 00:13:16.790
<v Alex>The article mentions analysts are now considering a range of outcomes,

280
00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:19.630
<v Alex>including his potential removal, which would be…

281
00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:20.458
<v Alex>quite a shift.

282
00:13:21.154 --> 00:13:25.742
<v Alex>I think the “central bank of AI” analogy is really underselling the risk here.

283
00:13:25.742 --> 00:13:28.036
<v Alex>It’s not about Nvidia failing outright,

284
00:13:28.036 --> 00:13:32.683
<v Alex>it’s about those financial commitments suddenly becoming a problem for a lot of

285
00:13:32.683 --> 00:13:33.624
<v Alex>other companies.

286
00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:36.669
<v Maya>I see it more as a symptom than a cause.

287
00:13:36.669 --> 00:13:39.663
<v Maya>Nvidia is just capitalizing on the existing demand,

288
00:13:39.663 --> 00:13:43.949
<v Maya>providing the financing that already exists somewhere else in the system.

289
00:13:44.645 --> 00:13:48.152
<v Alex>Still, a lot of money is tied up in their continued success.

290
00:13:48.152 --> 00:13:52.301
<v Alex>Huang’s situation adds a layer of unpredictable volatility, doesn't it?

291
00:13:52.997 --> 00:13:54.589
<v Maya>It's certainly distracting.

292
00:13:54.589 --> 00:13:57.833
<v Maya>But the board's confidence in him is important to note,

293
00:13:57.833 --> 00:14:00.015
<v Maya>it’s not like they’re in crisis mode.

294
00:14:00.711 --> 00:14:04.930
<v Alex>True, but that doesn't negate the potential for a broad-based repricing.

295
00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:06.337
<v Alex>It’s a delicate balance.

296
00:14:07.033 --> 00:14:10.771
<v Maya>My read is that the real concern isn’t Nvidia itself collapsing,

297
00:14:10.771 --> 00:14:15.676
<v Maya>but the interconnectedness of the whole AI buildout finally revealing its fragility.

298
00:14:16.372 --> 00:14:17.068
<v Alex>Makes sense.

299
00:14:17.764 --> 00:14:20.002
<v Maya>The worry isn't that Nvidia will fail,

300
00:14:20.002 --> 00:14:24.833
<v Maya>but that its success has masked a systemic reliance on a single point of potential

301
00:14:24.833 --> 00:14:25.304
<v Maya>failure.

302
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:27.005
<v Alex>That's the news.

303
00:14:27.005 --> 00:14:28.387
<v Alex>Time for The Takeaway.

304
00:14:29.121 --> 00:14:33.628
<v Maya>I think the common thread here is just how much money is sloshing around,

305
00:14:33.628 --> 00:14:36.160
<v Maya>enabling all this experimentation, right?

306
00:14:36.894 --> 00:14:41.907
<v Alex>I'll be watching to see how quickly those Adobe AI features actually ship – demos

307
00:14:41.907 --> 00:14:42.464
<v Alex>are easy.

308
00:14:43.198 --> 00:14:48.138
<v Maya>I'm curious to see if PSI can actually translate their AI discovery efforts into

309
00:14:48.138 --> 00:14:50.237
<v Maya>something beyond a research grant.

310
00:14:50.971 --> 00:14:55.663
<v Alex>Hold on, I'm not writing off Olaf Sakkers' approach just because it's niche.

311
00:14:55.663 --> 00:14:58.132
<v Alex>Someone’s gotta find the inefficiencies.

312
00:14:58.867 --> 00:15:03.118
<v Maya>I still maintain Nvidia’s financing web isn’t that surprising, Alex.

313
00:15:03.118 --> 00:15:03.743
<v Maya>It's just…

314
00:15:03.743 --> 00:15:04.681
<v Maya>infrastructure.

315
00:15:05.416 --> 00:15:10.251
<v Alex>I guess we’ll agree to disagree on how much that signifies about systemic risk.

316
00:15:10.985 --> 00:15:12.534
<v Maya>And that's The Breakdown.

317
00:15:12.534 --> 00:15:16.004
<v Maya>Every story we talked about is linked in the show notes.

318
00:15:16.738 --> 00:15:18.993
<v Alex>Next up is The Long Drive, tomorrow.

319
00:15:18.993 --> 00:15:21.936
<v Alex>One reminder: nothing here is financial advice.

320
00:15:21.936 --> 00:15:23.940
<v Alex>From Bits and Tickers, I'm Alex.

321
00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:24.817
<v Alex>Keep building.

322
00:15:25.552 --> 00:15:26.123
<v Maya>I'm Maya.

323
